A||N||10||peterh||low oil pressure||||1
Z||000000||peterh||10-25-1999||08:33 AM||||The oil pressure sending unit was leaking on my 86 V6 so I replaced it. Now the oil pressure cold is 65-70 but when hot falls to 20 or less (warning lite comes on) when stopped in traffic. If I shift into neutral the warning lite goes out. Is this something to worry about, or should I put some thicker oil in say 1 qt of 20W50. Pulling the pan for a new oil pump sounds like more work than I want right now. The engine does have 120K on it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-25-1999||09:08 AM||||If those numbers are acurate, then you definatly have a problem. could be either a shot pump, bad bypass valve(I think fiero uses one), or a bearing failing. when it warms up the bearing gap increases and lets more oil thru than it should.<P>at the very least do a oil/filter change. a bad oil filter can also do odd things. cut the old filter open and see if the media is full of metal. stir a magnet around in the old oil and see what it picks up. ((run the car around a bit to stir it all up good before the oil change.))||||reg||1
Z||000002||peterh||10-25-1999||01:15 PM||||Thanks, I'll try an oil change tonite.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||10-25-1999||02:40 PM||||As long as the pressure comes up,when it's revved up,then everything should be OK.<BR>GM cars have used a flow oriented oil system that works down to a very low pressure,where other cars would be clattering and banging around,about to die.<BR>If the lack of pressure was causing damage,you'd probably hear the lifters clattering first.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-25-1999||03:28 PM||||You didn't use thread tape on the new one did you. It's been known to throw off senders. you should only use pipe goop on them. goop won't interfere with sender getting a ground.<P>If it the only reason for replaceing the old sensor was leakage, then the new one could be bad. <P>next step is to make sure the numbers are accurate, and not a bad replacement sender. You can do that with a machanical gauge taped into an oil pasage. Pressure dropping at idle is normal, but it should not be dropping so low the light is on.<P>If the pressure really is that low, then flow likely isn't to good either. It needs to be checked out. <P>Even GM cars need more than X PSI. he's dropping under 20 and the light is on. I think the light only comes on under 10-15. If he really was dropping much lower than 10 the fuel interlock should cut out and kill the motor. I wouldn't count on that tho... I think it may not cut out unless your under 5.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroGT||10-25-1999||04:42 PM||||I remember reading in a Helms that the Fiero actually CAN run with no oil pressure (Not for long!). Some cars have the fuel shutoff feature, which is admittedly nice, but the Fiero is not one of these cars. I just had my GT running with the sending unit disconnected. It still ran fine. Gauge pegged past 80 psi. Then I capped the hole with an old sending unit and plugged the connector to my new sending unit. Reading on the gauge was ZERO and the light was on, but the motor ran just fine. Just an observation I found. Don't rely on the brainbox shutting things down for you in case of failure. You might be making an assumption that costs lots of $$$$$.<p>[This message has been edited by FieroGT (edited 10-25-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||squid||10-25-1999||06:06 PM||||Stock guages are not known for accuracy."T" off the other guage and add a mechanical one, atleast temporarily to check. Mine is between the shifter and the cigarette lighter.The rule of thumb is 10psi per 1000 rpm of use to prevent starvation. You could put a high volume pump in to combat your worn main and rod bearings but at higher revs you could pump the pan dry. Try thicker oil.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick||10-25-1999||06:40 PM||||<BR>In my '87 4-banger with 200,000 kilometers, I use <B>20W-50</B> in the summer, and <B>10W-40</B> in the winter. My oil pressure goes right to the top when I rev 'er up, and never goes below half way when it's idling, even on the hottest day of the summer.<P>Interestingly enough, I can't give you the readings in <B>psi</B> because the gauge is in some metric measurement. It's confusing as hell living in <B>Canada</B> and being subjected to both metric and standard measurements.||||reg||3
Z||000008||terryk||10-25-1999||07:17 PM||||My experience has been that the oil gauge is *reasonably* accurate. Your mains and cam bearings are probably getting loose and/or the oil pump gear-to-cover clearance has finally worn.<P>But......... if it's happy at idle and you get 10PSI/1000 RPM (to a limit of 55 PSI) leave it. The oil pressure will stop the pump at 4PSI (scary, but true).<P>I agree, no rattling of the lifters at idle and good pressure leave it.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Greg Piet||10-25-1999||07:24 PM||||I think the Haynes manual says to rev the engine at 2500 or 2000 rpm and oil pressure should be 25 to 45 psi (exact numbers unsure, but ballpark)||||reg||1
Z||000010||peterh||10-26-1999||08:37 AM||||This forum is great. Thanks for the info. I did not use teflon tape. The oil sender I got had a thin layer of some orange colored on the threads that I left on. I changed my oil (and filter) last nite to 3 qts of 10W30 and 1 of 20w50 and the oil lite has not come on this morning although the gauge goes down to approx 20 psi at hot idle. I have only put 200 miles on the car since getting it and do not know when the last owner changed the oil. I do not get any lifter or other engine noise. At approx. 2000 rpm the pressure is about 40psi. I was thinking of putting in either 10W40 at next change and/or another can of 20W50. Hopefully I can just enjoy driving the car daily for now. <BR>||||reg||1
